    "gold_answer": "Since $\\mathbf{M}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -17 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -20 \\end{pmatrix},$ we seek $p$ and $q$ such that\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} -17 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -20 \\end{pmatrix} = p \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\end{pmatrix} + q \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Thus, we want $p$ and $q$ to satisfy $2p + q = -17,$ $7p = 7,$ $-3p = -3,$ and $-p + q = -20.$  Solving, we find $(p,q) = \\boxed{(1,-19)}.$",
